---
/**
 * Data Compliance & Source Standards
 *
 * @description
 * 数据合规声明：数据验证分层口径（spec_source_type / data_tier / confidence）、来源分级标准、
 * 纠错流程、不虚构声明、数据端点许可（CC BY 4.0）与机器可读访问。
 */
import MarketingLayout from '@/layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '@/config';

const { legal, contact } = siteConfig;
const fullAddress = [
  contact.address.street,
  contact.address.city,
  contact.address.state,
  contact.address.country,
]
  .filter(Boolean)
  .join(', ');
---

<MarketingLayout
  title="Data Compliance & Source Standards"
  description="How EV Hub grades and dates its data sources, marks estimates versus official figures, corrects errors, and licenses its machine-readable datasets."
>
  <section class="py-section">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <header class="mb-12">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-text">Data Compliance &amp; Source Standards</h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-text-muted">Last updated: {legal.lastUpdated}</p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none dark:prose-invert">
        <p>
          {siteConfig.name} is a data-driven information platform. This page states, in plain terms, how we
          grade and date our sources, how we mark estimates versus official figures, how we correct errors,
          and what we will <em>not</em> do — fabricate data, ratings, or credentials. It should be read
          together with our <a href="/methodology/">Evidence &amp; Data Methodology</a>,{' '}
          <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a>, and <a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>1. Data verification tiering</h2>
        <p>
          Every vehicle record carries a source-type classification and a confidence level so readers can
          tell where a number came from and how much to trust it. The tiers we publish are:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Official specification</strong> — figures drawn from a manufacturer datasheet, an
          official price list, or a regulatory document (for example, an EU implementing regulation or a
          national tax schedule).</li>
          <li><strong>Manufacturer claim</strong> — figures sourced from manufacturer publications but not
          independently verified by us.</li>
          <li><strong>Independent test / third-party</strong> — figures attributed to whoever ran the test
          or published the measurement, never presented as our own.</li>
          <li><strong>Estimate</strong> — values we compute or benchmark (freight, certification,
          registration, and the landed-cost total). These are always labelled as estimates, never passed
          off as confirmed figures.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Where a field has no source, we leave it unstated (shown as "N/A") rather than invent a value.
          We do not convert between range standards (CLTC, WLTP, EPA) to fill gaps, because those
          standards measure differently and a conversion would be a fabricated number. Full detail is in
          our <a href="/methodology/">Evidence &amp; Data Methodology</a> and the{' '}
          <a href="/docs/data-provenance/">data provenance</a> document.
        </p>

        <h2>2. Source standards</h2>
        <p>We grade sources into four tiers and use the highest available tier for any figure stated as fact:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Tier 1 — Official and regulatory:</strong> EU implementing regulations and TARIC entries,
          national tax authorities, customs guidance, type-approval databases, official manufacturer
          datasheets and price lists.</li>
          <li><strong>Tier 2 — Authoritative media and analysts:</strong> established trade press and
          recognised analysts, attributed to the outlet rather than stated as our own finding.</li>
          <li><strong>Tier 3 — Industry and trade sources:</strong> freight benchmarks, homologation service
          providers, industry association publications, company statements. Treated as estimates.</li>
          <li><strong>Tier 4 — Community and user reports:</strong> forums and social posts, used only to find
          a lead worth checking, never as the sole basis for a factual claim or price.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          When two credible sources disagree, we prefer the higher tier and note the discrepancy rather
          than silently picking the number that reads better. Freshness is part of accuracy: data-bearing
          pages carry a verification date, so a stale figure is visible as stale.
        </p>

        <h2>3. What we will not fabricate</h2>
        <p>We commit, explicitly, not to do any of the following:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Fabricate or invent specifications, prices, range figures, tariffs, or ratings.</li>
          <li>Publish reviews, scores, or aggregate ratings we did not actually produce. Our comparison
          tool shows specifications only — it contains no ratings, reviews, or seller claims.</li>
          <li>Present a manufacturer's or a third party's test result as a test we ran.</li>
          <li>Convert between CLTC, WLTP, and EPA range figures to fill missing data.</li>
          <li>Claim certifications, awards, testing facilities, client relationships, or editorial
          staff we do not have.</li>
          <li>Blend specifications across trims or range standards into a single "representative" number.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Where our author's manufacturing-side experience informs an analysis, we label it as perspective,
          not as a measured result — see our <a href="/methodology/">Evidence &amp; Data Methodology</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>4. Estimates are labelled</h2>
        <p>
          Freight, certification, registration, and inland-transport figures are benchmark-based estimates,
          not quotes. Pages that use them mark them as estimates with their basis shown. The landed-cost
          calculator and comparison tool display reference estimates only; they are not quotes, offers, or
          predictions, as stated in our <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> and{' '}
          <a href="/landed-cost-methodology/">landed-cost methodology</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The distinction we draw is simple and we keep it consistent site-wide: an <strong>official</strong>{' '}
          figure (a duty rate, a VAT rate, a published specification) is shown as fact and dated; an{' '}
          <strong>estimate</strong> (freight, certification, the landed-cost total) is shown as an estimate
          and marked as such on the page. We do not let an estimate silently pose as an official figure, and
          we do not let an official figure be mistaken for a quote.
        </p>

        <h2>5. Correction process</h2>
        <p>We fix errors and say so. If you believe a figure, specification, or factual claim is wrong:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Email us at <a href={`mailto:${legal.legalEmail}`}>{legal.legalEmail}</a> with the page URL and
          the correction, ideally with a source we can check.</li>
          <li>We review the claim against the best available source. We acknowledge substantive correction
          reports and, where a claim is wrong, correct it rather than quietly swapping it out.</li>
          <li>Material corrections to dated figures or factual claims are recorded on the page, not silently
          changed.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          We also track corrections at the editorial level — see the{' '}
          <a href="/editorial-independence/">Editorial Independence Policy</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We do not publish "corrected" numbers that are really new estimates under an old date. When a
          correction changes a figure that other content depends on — a duty rate, a price, a range value —
          we update the dependent figures in the same pass so the site does not contradict itself.
        </p>

        <h2>6. Independence and disclosure</h2>
        <p>
          Our operator, {siteConfig.name} (Jinzhou Chengguang Power Source Co., Ltd.), works in the EV
          battery supply chain. That ownership is a commercial fact we disclose on our{' '}
          <a href="/editorial-independence/">Editorial Independence Policy</a> and{' '}
          <a href="/about/">About</a> page. It does not change our data rules: no brand, supplier, or
          commercial partner gets to approve, edit, or remove a figure because of a business relationship.
          A factual claim still has to stand on a citable source, and a conflict of interest is disclosed
          rather than hidden.
        </p>

        <h2>7. Machine-readable data and license</h2>
        <p>
          We publish structured datasets at our <code>/data/</code> endpoints (<code>vehicles</code>,{' '}
          <code>brands</code>, <code>tariffs</code>). These are the same single source of truth that
          powers the site — we do not maintain a separate, richer internal dataset.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>License:</strong> each dataset is released under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
          International license (CC BY 4.0). You may use, adapt, and redistribute it, including
          commercially, provided you give appropriate credit, link to the license, and indicate changes.</li>
          <li><strong>Scope:</strong> the license covers the structured data. Editorial articles, prose, and
          page design are not part of the dataset license and remain subject to our{' '}
          <a href="/copyright/">Copyright and Takedown Policy</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> the datasets reflect the site's published estimates and official
          figures at generation time. Where a value is an estimate, treat it as such.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Machine-readable access for search engines and AI assistants is permitted as described in our{' '}
          <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a> file and <a href="/robots.txt">robots.txt</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>8. Freshness, dating, and provenance fields</h2>
        <p>
          Every vehicle record carries provenance metadata that readers can inspect on the page: a source
          description (<code>data_source</code>), a verification date (<code>data_updated</code>), a review
          flag (<code>data_reviewed</code>), a tier (1–4), and a confidence level (<code>high</code> /{' '}
          <code>medium</code> / <code>low</code>). The landed-cost figure is always presented as an
          estimate and stamped with its verification date. Where a field has no confirmed source, it is
          rendered as "N/A" rather than filled in. We publish the field convention in full in the{' '}
          <a href="/docs/data-provenance/">data provenance</a> document.
        </p>
        <p>
          We do not run battery, range, or safety tests, and we do not present a figure as a measured
          result we produced. Where independent test data is cited, it is attributed to whoever ran the
          test. Our author's production-side experience is used only as labelled context and perspective,
          never as a substitute for a citable source on a factual claim.
        </p>

        <h2>9. Data access and attribution</h2>
        <p>
          You may access the structured datasets directly at the <code>/data/</code> endpoints, and you may
          use them under the CC BY 4.0 license described above. If you republish the data, credit{' '}
          {siteConfig.name} and link to the license, and do not imply that we endorse your use. If you
          substantially transform the data, say so. We ask that you not pass a landed-cost estimate on as
          a confirmed figure without carrying its estimate caveat.
        </p>
        <p>
          Access to the endpoints is provided as-is and without a service-level commitment; we may rate-limit
          or change the endpoints as needed to protect the site. Automated access should be polite, identify
          itself, and follow our <a href="/robots.txt">robots.txt</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>10. Related policies</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/methodology/">Evidence &amp; Data Methodology</a></li>
          <li><a href="/landed-cost-methodology/">Landed Cost Methodology</a></li>
          <li><a href="/editorial-independence/">Editorial Independence Policy</a></li>
          <li><a href="/copyright/">Copyright, Trademarks and Takedown Policy</a></li>
          <li><a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a></li>
          <li><a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a></li>
          <li><a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a></li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Questions about this policy: <a href={`mailto:${legal.legalEmail}`}>{legal.legalEmail}</a>.
          Address: {fullAddress}.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</MarketingLayout>
